"About how long does sunlight take to reach Earth?": "48f6e8a9ec8d",
"Beyond Neptune lie Pluto and many other icy worlds, and far beyond them, a vast cloud of comets that may reach a quarter of the way to the nearest star. The planets you found in Lesson 7 are tiny bright dots scattered across this enormous emptiness. Try the calculator below, build your own model at school, and walk the solar system for yourself.": "37e44666e2f8",
"Even light needs time to cross the solar system. Light moves 300,000 kilometers every second, yet sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach Earth, so the Sun you see is the Sun of eight minutes ago. Sunlight reaches Jupiter in about 43 minutes and Neptune in about four hours. Radio messages to rovers on Mars take between 3 and 22 minutes, depending on where the two planets are. Voyager 1, launched in 1977, is now so far away that on November 18, 2026, its signals will take a full day to reach us.": "a41542b61bf2",
"How did astronomers first get a good measurement of the distance to the Sun?": "615fa1211c22",
"How do we know such huge distances? Astronomers use a unit called the astronomical unit, or AU: the average distance from Earth to the Sun, about 150 million kilometers. Measuring it took centuries. In 1672, astronomers in Paris and in French Guiana measured the position of Mars at the same time, and the small difference between their views gave the first good estimate. In 1769, Captain Cook sailed to Tahiti to time Venus crossing the face of the Sun for the same purpose. Today, radar and spacecraft measure these distances to within a few meters.": "7bd874fe0a1c",
"Keep walking. Jupiter, the giant, is only a grape, 134 meters from the basketball. Saturn is a slightly smaller grape at 246 meters. Uranus and Neptune are peas, 495 and 776 meters out, almost two laps around a running track. On that scale, the nearest star after the Sun would be about 6,900 kilometers away, almost as far as Sydney is from Taiwan. Between all these tiny objects there is almost nothing at all. The solar system is mostly empty space.": "78888484fbb0",
"Most pictures of the solar system show the planets lined up close together, like beads on a string. Those pictures are useful, but they cannot be true to scale. To see why, shrink the Sun to the size of a basketball. Earth becomes a sesame seed, about 26 meters away, and the Moon is a grain of sand less than seven centimeters from it. Mercury, Venus, and Mars are even smaller seeds, scattered within 40 meters of the ball.": "3728e1b2977c",
