e Chinese word for universe, yuzhou, joins two ideas: yu, all of space in every direction, and zhou, all of time from the past to the future. The astronomer Zhang Heng, almost 1,900 years ago, pictured the sky as an egg with Earth as the yolk inside. Telescopes later added two planets no one had seen before: Uranus in 1781 and Neptune in 1846. Neptune was first found with mathematics, from the way it tugged on Uranus, and only then spotted in the sky. Beyond Neptune lie Pluto and many other icy worlds, and far beyond them, a vast cloud of comets that may reach a quarter of the way to the nearest star. The planets you found in Lesson 7 are tiny bright dots scattered across this enormous emptiness. Try the calculator below, build your own model at school, and walk the solar system for yourself.": "dff035876620",
